INFO:agent.loop:\u26a1 Executing tool: 'knowledge_agent' with arguments: {"source_title": "Pandora Papers reporting on King Abdullah II property network", "source_url": "https://www.theguardian.com/news/2021/oct/03/king-of-jordan-hidden-property-empire-worth-more-than-100m-pandora-papers-reveal", "text": "Benchmark sources report that leaked Pandora Papers documents linked Jordan's King Abdullah II to an offshore property network. The Guardian reports an international luxury property portfolio worth well in excess of $100m, with properties from Malibu, California, to Washington, DC and central London; ownership was disguised through offshore companies incorporated in the British Virgin Islands. It reports three Malibu properties acquired through Nabisco Holdings SA, Setara Limited and Timara Limited; seven luxury UK properties purchased 2003-2011, including three in Belgravia, with estimated current value about £28m; three Washington DC condominiums bought in the prior two years for $13.8m; and a Malibu mega-mansion bought August 2014 for $33.5m, followed by two neighboring properties. A February 2017 Alcogal memorandum identified a Jordanian client called Abdullah Al Hussein, born 30 January 1962, address Raghadan Palace, as beneficial owner of 16 companies holding assets in the US, UK and Jersey. The Guardian says Alcogal staff discussed avoiding identification as a politically exposed person and referred to the final beneficiary as 'you know who'; Alcogal denied deliberately failing to identify a PEP and said identity details were recorded in the BVI beneficial ownership system in November 2017. DW reports 14 houses and flats in the US and UK, total more than €91m/$106m, through 36 front companies registered in tax havens, and says trust-firm sources referred to him as the final beneficiary or 'You know who' from Jordan. Al Jazeera reports three Malibu beachfront mansions purchased through three offshore companies for $68m, plus Washington and UK properties, and says the documents show a network of offshore companies and tax havens. The royal palace and lawyers deny wrongdoing and say all properties and related expenses were personally funded, not by the state budget, treasury, public monies, aid, grants or loans; DLA Piper said offshore ownership was common for privacy/security and denied impropriety. Sources note offshore ownership is not itself illegal, and no evidence of wrongdoing was reported by the Guardian; DW assessed purchases as probably not illegal but potentially at odds with transparency principles. Jurisdictions explicitly reported include British Virgin Islands, UK, US and Jersey for Abdullah-linked assets; broader Pandora Papers provider jurisdictions include Panama, Belize, Cyprus, UAE, Singapore and Switzerland, but the sources do not establish that all were part of Abdullah's personal network."}
